w: I asked the Lord that I might grow In faith, and love, and ev- ery grace;
w: ’Twas He who taught me thus to pray, And He, I trust, has ans- wered prayer!
w: I hoped that in some fa- vored hour, At once He’d an- swer my re- quest;
w: Yea more, with His own hand He seemed In- tent to ag- gra- vate my woe;
w: Lord, why is this, I trem- bling cried, Wilt thou pur- sue Thy worm to death?
w: These in- ward tri- als I em- ploy, From self, and pride, to set thee free;
G A B | d2 c | B2 A | G3 | A B c | e2 d | c2 B | A3 |]
w: Might more of His sal- va- tion know, And seek, more ear- nest- ly, His face.
w: But it has been in such a way, As al- most drove me to des- pair.
w: And by His love’s con- strain- ing pow’r, Sub- due my sins, and give me rest.
w: Crossed all the fair de- signs I schemed, Blas- ted my gourds, and laid me low.
w: ’Tis in this way, the Lord re- plied, I ans- wer prayer for grace and faith.
w: And break thy schemes of earth- ly joy, That thou may’st find thy all in Me.
